\section{Introduction}
The California condor is a large scavenger native to the southwestern United States, identified by its $9\frac{1}{2}$-foot wingspan and bald pink head. Scavenging birds such as these play a critical part in their native ecosystems, preventing the spread of disease by disposing of carrion before deadly bacteria are exposed to the broader environment. Despite thriving in North America for thousands of years, the introduction of the insecticide DDT in the $20^{\text{th}}$ century caused their numbers to plummet into the tens by the 1980s. To prevent outright extinction, all 22 of the estimated birds remaining in the wild were captured and placed into captive breeding programs, where their numbers gradually recovered enough to permit managed reintroduction into their native mountain ranges.

In the wild, California condors breed slowly and deliberately. After reaching sexual maturity at around 8 years of age, a mating pair produces a single egg that is incubated for approximately 6 months. After hatching, the chick remains dependent on its parents for another year. Even after fledgling independence, wild condors face severe natural and anthropogenic threats—such as lead poisoning from ammunition—leading to substantial mortality. 

In captivity, researchers accelerate the productivity of mating pairs through an intervention called \emph{double-clutching}. During this process, the first egg laid by a pair is removed and artificially incubated by researchers. Lacking viable offspring in the nest, the mating pair frequently lays a replacement egg, effectively doubling annual reproduction. 

This paper develops a system dynamics model implemented in MATLAB to track coupled captive and wild condor populations across single-year age cohorts. We evaluate the impact of age structure, double-clutching, release quotas, environmental carrying capacities, and varying stage-specific mortality rates to assess the long-term viability of reintroduction programs.

\section{Methodology}

\subsection{System Dynamics Overview}
The model tracks population distributions across discrete age cohorts using stock-and-flow dynamics. Stock variables represent captive and wild condor counts at each year of age. The primary flow between chronological cohorts is maturation, constrained by cohort-specific mortality rates. Juvenile release flows transfer birds aged 1 year from captivity to wild stocks, governed by captive carrying capacities and annual transfer policies.

\begin{figure}[H]
