\section{Conclusions}
The comparative simulations highlight the sensitivity of wild California condor recovery to juvenile survival and continued artificial management:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Double-clutching leverage:} Captive double-clutching effectively counterbalances the delayed sexual maturity (7–8 years) and low natural clutch sizes of the species, providing a continuous influx of juveniles to wild habitats.
    \item \textbf{Mortality bottlenecks:} Elevated first-year mortality ($d_1 = 0.659$) severely curtails self-sustaining population growth in the wild, indicating that wild populations remain dependent on captive supplementation until adult breeding stocks reach critical mass.
    \item \textbf{Cessation risks:} Early termination of captive breeding programs (Scenario F) exposes wild populations to rapid decline if wild mortality remains high, demonstrating the necessity of long-term demographic support until ecological threats (e.g., lead toxicosis) are mitigated.
\end{itemize}

